WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.326
This is the executive
briefing for Book 07 of the

3
00:00:04.326 --> 00:00:08.424
Federal Modernization
Workbook, PKI and certificates.

4
00:00:08.424 --> 00:00:11.498
It's for the people who decide: the CIO,

5
00:00:11.498 --> 00:00:15.800
the CISO, the PKI owner
and authorizing officials.

6
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:17.225
The argument is short.

7
00:00:17.225 --> 00:00:21.969
Certificates prove who's who in every
other migration, and they keep working

8
00:00:21.969 --> 00:00:26.637
until the day they expire, so they fail
on their own clock, not the directory's.

9
00:00:26.637 --> 00:00:30.435
There's no single replacement for
the old certificate authority;

10
00:00:30.435 --> 00:00:33.600
the target is four issuers
and one lifecycle platform.

11
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.350
And the move runs as its own program,
with the old authority retired last.

12
00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:42.725
Each decision slide names the decision,
its owner and when it's needed.

13
00:00:42.725 --> 00:00:44.340
Let's start with why.

14
00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:49.965
Every other migration changes which
system answers the question, who is this?

15
00:00:49.965 --> 00:00:52.452
Certificates are how
the answer gets proved.

16
00:00:52.452 --> 00:00:55.877
And a certificate isn't a record
you move with the directory.

17
00:00:55.877 --> 00:01:00.205
It's a signed statement that's already
been handed out, sitting in devices,

18
00:01:00.205 --> 00:01:04.316
smart cards, servers and trust
stores nobody remembers configuring.

19
00:01:04.316 --> 00:01:10.290
It keeps working, unchanged, until it expires,
and then whatever relied on it stops.

20
00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:16.827
Domain controllers, Wi-Fi, VPN, server
encryption and PIV sign-in all lean on it.

21
00:01:16.827 --> 00:01:20.253
Each book in this workbook
migrates its own consumers.

22
00:01:20.253 --> 00:01:23.327
None of them owns the issuer
underneath all of them.

23
00:01:23.327 --> 00:01:26.550
That's the problem, and it
runs on a different clock.

24
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.700
There are two clocks here.

25
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:32.200
Directory work fails at cutover,
where someone is watching.

26
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:36.925
Certificate work fails at expiry,
months later and far from its cause.

27
00:01:36.925 --> 00:01:39.735
So a program that measures
progress by cutover

28
00:01:39.735 --> 00:01:42.400
dates reports success
right up to the outage.

29
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.500
And the cloud options only
cover part of the estate.

30
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:50.516
Cloud PKI issues only to Intune-managed
devices, which don't include servers,

31
00:01:50.516 --> 00:01:54.975
and domain controllers need
certificates for as long as they exist.

32
00:01:54.975 --> 00:01:59.986
The decision for the sponsor and the CIO:
run the move off the old certificate

33
00:01:59.986 --> 00:02:04.838
authority as its own program, with its
own owner, sequenced by expiry dates.

34
00:02:04.838 --> 00:02:07.440
And those dates are
getting closer together.

35
00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:11.378
The rules are moving under
certificates already issued.

36
00:02:11.378 --> 00:02:16.218
Public TLS lifetimes are
falling on a fixed schedule:

37
00:02:16.218 --> 00:02:21.939
200 days now, 100 days from March
2027, and 47 days from March 2029.

38
00:02:21.939 --> 00:02:25.960
On the book's arithmetic, a thousand
certificates means about seven

39
00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:29.765
renewals every working day today,
and about thirty-one at the end.

40
00:02:29.765 --> 00:02:34.390
The mapping rules for sign-in certificates
already changed, with no way back.

41
00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:39.403
And the algorithms have a horizon, with
federal post-quantum deadlines in 2030.

42
00:02:39.403 --> 00:02:42.773
So the decision for the CISO:
set lifetimes by policy,

43
00:02:42.773 --> 00:02:47.064
and require automated renewal for
every class before they shorten.

44
00:02:47.064 --> 00:02:50.590
No certificate should depend on
a person remembering a date.

45
00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:53.150
First, we need to know what we have.

46
00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.652
Before any certificate moves,

47
00:02:55.652 --> 00:03:00.278
the program establishes what the old
certificate authority actually holds up:

48
00:03:00.278 --> 00:03:03.713
every authority, every template,
every certificate issued,

49
00:03:03.713 --> 00:03:05.675
and every system that trusts them.

50
00:03:05.675 --> 00:03:08.225
Then each template gets
one of four verdicts.

51
00:03:08.225 --> 00:03:10.738
Retire it if its consumer is going away.

52
00:03:10.738 --> 00:03:13.750
Migrate it if Intune or
Entra can replace it.

53
00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:18.250
Bridge it if it depends on Active
Directory itself, like domain controllers.

54
00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:23.162
And retain it only if nothing else can
serve it, with an owner and a review date.

55
00:03:23.162 --> 00:03:27.853
The decision for the PKI administrators:
every template classified,

56
00:03:27.853 --> 00:03:31.062
with a named owner, in the
plan's first four weeks.

57
00:03:31.062 --> 00:03:34.850
And before any of it moves,
what stays has to be safe.

58
00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.437
A certificate authority can mint
credentials the whole estate trusts.

59
00:03:39.437 --> 00:03:43.154
So one over-privileged account
or a single misconfigured

60
00:03:43.154 --> 00:03:46.250
template can undo every
other identity control.

61
00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:49.742
And the new cloud issuer will
chain to this same hierarchy,

62
00:03:49.742 --> 00:03:51.925
inheriting whatever's wrong with it.

63
00:03:51.925 --> 00:03:56.487
That's why hardening is the first technical
workstream, not cleanup at the end.

64
00:03:56.487 --> 00:04:01.395
Eight well-known misconfiguration classes
get checked, four of them rated critical,

65
00:04:01.395 --> 00:04:05.601
and auditing gets turned on, because
by default there's no evidence at all.

66
00:04:05.601 --> 00:04:10.137
The decision for the PKI owner and
the security team: hold the gate.

67
00:04:10.137 --> 00:04:15.225
Nothing migrates until every critical
and high finding is fixed and re-tested.

68
00:04:15.225 --> 00:04:17.060
Then, the target.

69
00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:21.085
There's no single replacement for
the old certificate authority.

70
00:04:21.085 --> 00:04:24.822
The target is four issuers,
each serving what only it can.

71
00:04:24.822 --> 00:04:28.297
Cloud PKI for devices Intune manages.

72
00:04:28.297 --> 00:04:32.495
A smaller, hardened internal
authority for domain controllers,

73
00:04:32.495 --> 00:04:34.159
servers and network gear.

74
00:04:34.159 --> 00:04:38.935
The Federal PKI for PIV cards,
which the migration doesn't touch.

75
00:04:38.935 --> 00:04:42.473
And a public authority for
names the internet has to trust.

76
00:04:42.473 --> 00:04:47.435
Entra ID sits beside them all, checking
certificates, never issuing them.

77
00:04:47.435 --> 00:04:51.889
The decision for the sponsor and the
CIO: adopt this four-issuer target,

78
00:04:51.889 --> 00:04:55.422
anchoring the cloud issuer to
the root you already have.

79
00:04:55.422 --> 00:04:58.500
That choice of root
carries a cost of its own.

80
00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:00.712
Here's the root decision.

81
00:05:00.712 --> 00:05:03.441
A brand-new cloud root means every server,

82
00:05:03.441 --> 00:05:06.812
gateway and device has to
learn to trust a new chain.

83
00:05:06.812 --> 00:05:11.592
Anchoring the cloud issuer under the root
you already have keeps that trust intact,

84
00:05:11.592 --> 00:05:15.887
uses one of the tenant's three cloud
authorities, and leaves an easy way back.

85
00:05:15.887 --> 00:05:19.676
That's the program's recommendation
for an established hierarchy.

86
00:05:19.676 --> 00:05:21.850
But it carries one lasting cost.

87
00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:25.769
The offline root's revocation
list now has to stay published at

88
00:05:25.769 --> 00:05:29.387
a plain web address that Entra
ID can reach, or sign-ins fail.

89
00:05:29.387 --> 00:05:34.712
So the decision for the PKI administrators,
inside the security office's window:

90
00:05:34.712 --> 00:05:38.988
run that list as a production
service, with every ceremony dated.

91
00:05:38.988 --> 00:05:41.160
Servers still need an issuer.

92
00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:45.351
Microsoft's issuers don't
renew server certificates,

93
00:05:45.351 --> 00:05:47.697
public certificates or appliances.

94
00:05:47.697 --> 00:05:50.587
Something has to find those
and renew them on time,

95
00:05:50.587 --> 00:05:52.560
and that's the lifecycle platform.

96
00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:55.743
The program uses CyberArk
Certificate Manager,

97
00:05:55.743 --> 00:05:59.085
from a vendor it already has,
and adds no second tool.

98
00:05:59.085 --> 00:06:03.890
Server certificates for internal names
come from the retained internal authority,

99
00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:07.460
requested and renewed through
the platform instead of by hand.

100
00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:09.510
One fact shaped the deployment.

101
00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:14.362
As of October 2026 the platform's
cloud edition had no FedRAMP listing,

102
00:06:14.362 --> 00:06:19.047
so the program runs the self-hosted
edition inside its own boundary.

103
00:06:19.047 --> 00:06:22.261
The decision for the sponsor
and CIO: confirm that,

104
00:06:22.261 --> 00:06:25.160
and revisit it when the
Marketplace changes.

105
00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:26.820
Then there's PIV.

106
00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:31.157
PIV is the one part the
migration doesn't re-issue.

107
00:06:31.157 --> 00:06:35.720
Those certificates still come from the
Federal PKI through the card system.

108
00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:40.851
What changes is who trusts them: the federal
root has to reach devices and servers,

109
00:06:40.851 --> 00:06:44.833
and Entra ID has to check each
card against its revocation list.

110
00:06:44.833 --> 00:06:46.219
Two choices matter.

111
00:06:46.219 --> 00:06:50.394
Every authority Entra trusts can
sign in any user it's bound to,

112
00:06:50.394 --> 00:06:52.358
so bindings have to be strong.

113
00:06:52.358 --> 00:06:55.231
And a PIN-protected card
counts as multifactor,

114
00:06:55.231 --> 00:07:00.169
while a software certificate doesn't, so
derived credentials need their own rule.

115
00:07:00.169 --> 00:07:04.561
The decision for the CISO: require
high-affinity bindings in production,

116
00:07:04.561 --> 00:07:08.232
and send any weaker binding kept
to the authorizing official.

117
00:07:08.232 --> 00:07:10.230
So who owns all of this?

118
00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:15.432
In most estates, the certificate
authority belongs to whoever installed it,

119
00:07:15.432 --> 00:07:18.542
and by default the domain's
top administrators run it.

120
00:07:18.542 --> 00:07:20.017
Nobody decided that.

121
00:07:20.017 --> 00:07:24.087
In the target, the estate spreads
across more systems, not fewer,

122
00:07:24.087 --> 00:07:28.468
each with administrators holding
a piece of what the agency trusts.

123
00:07:28.468 --> 00:07:31.643
So the program separates who
decides from who does the work.

124
00:07:31.643 --> 00:07:36.304
The CISO sets issuance policy,
trust anchors and binding strength.

125
00:07:36.304 --> 00:07:39.830
The authorizing official accepts
every standing exception.

126
00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:44.892
The sponsor and CIO decide the hierarchy
and when old authorities retire.

127
00:07:44.892 --> 00:07:49.659
And their decision here: name one renewal
owner for every certificate class,

128
00:07:49.659 --> 00:07:51.929
recorded in the lifecycle platform.

129
00:07:51.929 --> 00:07:56.370
Because an expiry is almost always
an ownership failure first.

130
00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:00.840
Certificate power is granted
in four separate places:

131
00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:06.295
the old authority's own permissions, Intune,
Entra ID, and the lifecycle platform.

132
00:08:06.295 --> 00:08:08.107
That's where hidden risk sits.

133
00:08:08.107 --> 00:08:11.982
Several everyday console clicks
are really risk decisions.

134
00:08:11.982 --> 00:08:14.370
Leaving a template published just in case.

135
00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:18.203
Trusting an authority in Entra
without a revocation list,

136
00:08:18.203 --> 00:08:20.758
so revoked certificates still sign in.

137
00:08:20.758 --> 00:08:22.870
Leaving bindings at the weak default.

138
00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:25.228
And the platform's own account can approve

139
00:08:25.228 --> 00:08:27.720
certificates for every
template it reaches.

140
00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:31.466
The decision for the CISO: map
every team in all four systems,

141
00:08:31.466 --> 00:08:34.913
keep privileged roles eligible
rather than permanent,

142
00:08:34.913 --> 00:08:39.408
and make sure no one can both change
and approve what an authority issues.

143
00:08:39.408 --> 00:08:41.410
Then the migration itself.

144
00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:45.277
The migration runs as six waves: assess,

145
00:08:45.277 --> 00:08:50.147
build the foundation, pilot,
scale, cut over, and decommission.

146
00:08:50.147 --> 00:08:54.500
Each wave's exit gate is the next
one's entry gate, and the durations,

147
00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:59.659
about forty-four weeks for a single
forest, are planning figures, not promises.

148
00:08:59.659 --> 00:09:02.673
The rule that matters
most is about going back.

149
00:09:02.673 --> 00:09:05.726
No wave starts until its
rollback has been rehearsed,

150
00:09:05.726 --> 00:09:09.197
and no rollback depends on
something that same wave retired.

151
00:09:09.197 --> 00:09:12.915
That's why the old automatic
enrollment stays on through the pilot,

152
00:09:12.915 --> 00:09:16.497
and federation stays restorable
for thirty days after cutover.

153
00:09:16.497 --> 00:09:22.123
The decision for the sponsor and CIO: approve
each gate before the next wave starts.

154
00:09:22.123 --> 00:09:25.150
During all of this, both systems are live.

155
00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:29.125
For most of the migration,
both systems are production.

156
00:09:29.125 --> 00:09:33.223
Devices carry certificates from
both, servers trust both chains,

157
00:09:33.223 --> 00:09:36.713
and two sets of revocation
lists have to stay reachable.

158
00:09:36.713 --> 00:09:38.376
Five rules keep that safe.

159
00:09:38.376 --> 00:09:41.013
Each use case gets one issuer at a time.

160
00:09:41.013 --> 00:09:43.374
Trust goes up before issuance moves.

161
00:09:43.374 --> 00:09:46.825
Revocation is reachable
wherever validation happens.

162
00:09:46.825 --> 00:09:49.387
Nothing is revoked early to force a move.

163
00:09:49.387 --> 00:09:51.676
And every move has a tested way back.

164
00:09:51.676 --> 00:09:54.331
The book's warning is worth remembering:

165
00:09:54.331 --> 00:09:57.712
most coexistence outages
are revocation outages.

166
00:09:57.712 --> 00:10:01.938
A list Entra ID can't reach within
ten seconds fails the sign-in.

167
00:10:01.938 --> 00:10:05.920
After the migration, the work
becomes keeping it all renewed.

168
00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:08.470
Migration ends on a date.

169
00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:09.820
Certificates don't.

170
00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:11.689
Every one reaches its expiry,

171
00:10:11.689 --> 00:10:16.283
and every authority must keep publishing
a revocation list until then.

172
00:10:16.283 --> 00:10:20.007
So the program watches for trouble
while it can still be fixed:

173
00:10:20.007 --> 00:10:24.754
any certificate close to expiry with no
renewal path, anything issued outside

174
00:10:24.754 --> 00:10:29.282
policy, a trust store holding something
unapproved, a list about to go stale.

175
00:10:29.282 --> 00:10:32.671
The evidence is kept weekly,
because an assessor asks how

176
00:10:32.671 --> 00:10:35.770
long a finding stayed open,
not just whether it's open.

177
00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:37.282
One choice needs care.

178
00:10:37.282 --> 00:10:40.345
Making a valid certificate
a condition of access

179
00:10:40.345 --> 00:10:43.108
turns a failed renewal
into a locked-out user.

180
00:10:43.108 --> 00:10:46.219
So the CISO decides
the grace period first.

181
00:10:46.219 --> 00:10:48.730
Then the old authority can retire.

182
00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:53.705
Retirement is the last wave, and it
happens one authority at a time.

183
00:10:53.705 --> 00:10:57.626
The gate is strict: cutover done,
thirty days with no incidents,

184
00:10:57.626 --> 00:11:00.855
and everything that stays
documented and running.

185
00:11:00.855 --> 00:11:04.567
Even then, the addresses where
certificates check their status

186
00:11:04.567 --> 00:11:08.280
keep serving until the last
certificate they cover has expired.

187
00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:12.668
Under the recommended design, the root
that signed the cloud issuer stays.

188
00:11:12.668 --> 00:11:17.520
And the authority's records carry their
own federal retention, measured in years,

189
00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:21.430
so the records officer signs off
before any archive is destroyed.

190
00:11:21.430 --> 00:11:26.194
The decision for the sponsor and CIO:
retire each authority only after its

191
00:11:26.194 --> 00:11:31.192
gate passes, keeping one for domain
controllers until the last of them is gone.

192
00:11:31.192 --> 00:11:32.860
Now, the risks.

193
00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:35.760
The risks fall into three groups.

194
00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:40.066
Expiry: a certificate nobody owned,
one that was never inventoried,

195
00:11:40.066 --> 00:11:43.510
or the enrollment server's
own certificates lapsing.

196
00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:48.410
Revocation: a list Entra can't reach fails
every sign-in for that authority, while

197
00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:53.385
an authority trusted with no list at all
lets revoked certificates keep working.

198
00:11:53.385 --> 00:11:57.442
And trust: weak bindings let a
look-alike certificate sign in,

199
00:11:57.442 --> 00:12:01.985
and the new cloud issuer inherits
any weakness in the old hierarchy.

200
00:12:01.985 --> 00:12:04.998
Temporary exceptions
are the CISO's to grant.

201
00:12:04.998 --> 00:12:08.973
Standing ones, with no end
date, are risk acceptances.

202
00:12:08.973 --> 00:12:11.905
So the decision for the
authorizing official:

203
00:12:11.905 --> 00:12:15.223
accept each standing exception
explicitly, or don't.

204
00:12:15.223 --> 00:12:17.440
Let's pull the decisions together.

205
00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:19.765
So, the decisions.

206
00:12:19.765 --> 00:12:22.800
The sponsor and CIO run
this as its own program,

207
00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:26.714
adopt the four-issuer target
anchored to the existing root,

208
00:12:26.714 --> 00:12:32.066
confirm the self-hosted lifecycle platform,
name a renewal owner for every class,

209
00:12:32.066 --> 00:12:37.178
approve each wave's gate, and retire each
authority only after its gate passes.

210
00:12:37.178 --> 00:12:42.289
The CISO sets lifetimes and automated
renewal, requires strong bindings,

211
00:12:42.289 --> 00:12:46.328
owns one access model, and sets
the compliance grace period.

212
00:12:46.328 --> 00:12:50.978
The PKI team classifies every template
and runs the root's revocation list

213
00:12:50.978 --> 00:12:55.703
as a production service, and with the
security team holds the hardening gate.

214
00:12:55.703 --> 00:12:59.152
The authorizing official
accepts each standing exception.

215
00:12:59.152 --> 00:13:00.841
It starts with the inventory.

216
00:13:00.841 --> 00:13:04.990
Book 07 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

217
00:13:04.990 --> 00:13:05.800
Thank you.
